      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the prevalence and weight gain as a risk factor of hypertension (HTN) at three years of age and older among extremely low birth weight (ELBW) infants."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective cohort study of all ELBW infants born and followed up at our institution over 15 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "204 infants were followed up for [median (inter-quartile range)] 10.2 years (6.9-13.0). Their birth weight and gestational age were (mean±standard deviation) 758±149\u200ag and 26.4±2.1 weeks respectively. At 3 years of age and older, the prevalence of HTN was 7.3% (15/204), and the prevalence of obesity was 13.2% (27/204). Obesity was more prevalent in children with than without HTN [33% (5/15) vs. 12% (22/189); p\u200a=\u200a0.01]. In a regression analysis, body mass index (BMI) percentile at 3 years of age and older (p\u200a=\u200a0.04) and increase in weight Z scores since birth (p\u200a=\u200a0.008) were associated with a higher systolic blood pressure (SBP; R\u200a=\u200a0.386, p\u200a< \u200a0.001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Obesity is prevalent in ELBW infants at three years of age and older, and it is significantly associated with hypertension. An increase in BMI percentile at ≥3 years of age, and the rate of weight gain since birth among ELBW infants are associated with an increase in SBP."
        }
      ]},
